In my opinion, the Nethack story adds very little to the game.  Gary
Gygax circa first adition AD&D said that, in his opinion, storytelling
has little to do with a roleplaying game, and Nethack subscribes to
that philosophy.

Up until 3.1, or soon before it, there was no such thing as Moloch,
the lower levels were still Hell, the Wizard of Yendor still had the
Amulet of Yendor (which makes a certain amount of sense: they're both
*of Yendor, duh), Ludios was Fort Knox and so forth.  Yes, things were
a bit sillier back then, and perhaps that was improper.  Certainly
many things were improved with 3.1, but the theme of the game and the
player's motivation were a step backwards.

Take a look at ADOM's story, in my opinion the worst thing about that
game.  Whenever I hear someone talk about their quest to reach the
"Ultimate Chaos God" ending, where the player's character gets to
become such, I cringe inside.  That is not good writing.

D&D characters have never really needed that much reason to trapise
through monster dens and slay staples of xenobiology.  By leaving the
motivation vague, the player can supply whatever purpose he wishes,
and it'll probably a lot more supportive of exploring a big
underground space that contains dragons, nymphs, soldiers and kitchen
sinks anyway.

Sorry for taking your simple request and making it the launching point
of a tirade.  Just somethin's been on my mind.

